"""Prose sections (stage 4 draft) for Paper 1, 'How the 1982–83 El Niño developed'.

build(c) returns section dicts; c(*keys) renders numbered citation links. Every number here is taken from
the timeline, evidence table, monthly table or the diagnostics in data/papers/1982/; nothing new is introduced.
"""


def build(c):
    S = []
    S.append({"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "html": f"""<p>The 1982–83 El Niño is the strongest event in the Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI) record, peaking at +2.40 in DJF 1983, and it was neither forecast nor recognized as it began. We reconstruct its development from contemporaneous ocean observations, later reconstructions and reanalysis diagnostics, and grade each proposed cause by the strength of event-specific evidence. The equatorial Pacific entered 1982 recharged: warm water volume was positive throughout 1981 and reached the 96th percentile of 1980–2025 months in March 1982, while RONI was only +0.11 (DJF). Western-Pacific trade winds weakened from February. A strong Madden–Julian oscillation (MJO) crossed into the western Pacific in December 1981, and a documented westerly wind burst occurred on 6–16 May 1982. The decisive forcing, however, came in June: sustained westerlies west of the Date Line, preceded by cross-equatorial southerly flow east of Australia, forced a downwelling Kelvin wave that raised Christmas Island sea level by about 25 cm in July. Anomalous eastward advection by Kelvin-wave currents then produced equatorial warming in July, before the coast warmed. This reversed the order seen in the pre-1982 composite, because the zonal SST gradient is strong in mid-year. Bjerknes feedback amplified the warming from August to December; recharge–discharge dynamics and two draining episodes governed decay and lingering far-eastern warmth into mid-1983. The MJO is a probable contributor to growth in June–July (grade B, one reconstruction study) but its role in initiation is unresolved. El Chichón (29 March–4 April 1982) erupted after recharge and trade weakening had begun; its contribution to initiation is not supported and its contribution to growth is unresolved. The coupled positive Indian Ocean Dipole of September–November 1982 was largely a response to the Pacific; model studies suggest, without consensus, that it may have intensified the late-1982 peak.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "findings", "title": "Main findings", "html": f"""<ol>
<li><b>Precondition (grade B; medium confidence).</b> A recharged equatorial warm-water reservoir preceded onset: warm water volume +0.6 to +2.1 × 10¹⁴ m³ through 1981 and +2.15 × 10¹⁴ m³ in March 1982; upper-ocean heat content east of the Date Line rose to about +0.9 °C by March {c('cpc', 'meinen2000', 'wyrtki1985b')}.</li>
<li><b>Trigger of the vigorous phase (grade B; medium–high confidence).</b> Sustained equatorial westerlies from June 1982, fed by cross-equatorial southerly flow at 150°–160°E, forced the downwelling Kelvin wave of June–July {c('harrison1984', 'harrisonschopf1984', 'rk2007')}.</li>
<li><b>Onset mechanism (grade A−; high confidence).</b> Kelvin-wave-induced eastward advection acting on the strong mid-year zonal SST gradient explains why warming began on the equator in July rather than at the South American coast {c('harrisonschopf1984', 'halpern1983', 'firing1983')}.</li>
<li><b>Amplifier (grade B; high confidence for the observation, general theory for the mechanism).</b> Trades, SOI, convection and SST strengthened together from August to December 1982 {c('cpc', 'bjerknes1969')}.</li>
<li><b>MJO (grade B for June–July growth; unresolved for initiation).</b> MJO-like activity occurred in December 1981, late March 1982 and late April–June 1982; one reconstruction links the May–June MJO to the June–July Kelvin wave {c('rk2007', 'weickmannkhalsa1990', 'bergman2001')}.</li>
<li><b>El Chichón (grade D for initiation, C for growth).</b> Timing argues against a trigger role; model pathways for amplification disagree and cannot be separated from El Niño's own signals in the observations {c('predybaylo2020', 'chai2020', 'kim2025', 'dee2020')}.</li>
<li><b>Indian Ocean (largely a consequence).</b> The coupled IOD came after Pacific warming was established and was as large as ENSO alone predicts; any feedback onto the Pacific peak is model-dependent {c('luo2010', 'hameed2018', 'ohbaueda2007')}.</li>
</ol>"""})

    S.append({"id": "intro", "title": "1. Introduction", "html": f"""<p>Before 1982 the canonical description of El Niño was the composite of six warm episodes since 1949 compiled by Rasmusson and Carpenter {c('rc1982')}. In that composite the equatorial easterlies west of the Date Line strengthen in the months before an event, give way to westerly anomalies in the preceding October–November, and SST anomalies along the coast of Ecuador and Peru begin rising around December–January, peak around April–June and then spread westward into the central Pacific. The 1982–83 event did not follow this sequence. Strong warming appeared first along the equator in July 1982 and reached the coast only afterwards, a timing and pattern described as quite different from most earlier events {c('harrisonschopf1984', 'cane1983')}. The event was compared with the composite as it developed {c('rw1983')}, but coupled ocean–atmosphere forecasts did not exist until the 1986 event {c('czd1986')}, and the observing system available in 1982 lacked the moored array, altimetry and profiling floats on which later diagnoses rely.</p>
<p>This paper asks how the event developed: which pre-existing conditions made it possible, what started its vigorous phase, what amplified it, and how it interacted with intraseasonal variability, a major volcanic eruption and the Indian Ocean. We treat each factor separately, state the timing of its influence, and grade the evidence (A–D) according to whether it rests on event-specific observations, event-specific model or budget studies, or only on composites and general theory. Where published studies disagree we report the disagreement rather than resolve it by preference.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "methods", "title": "2. Definitions, data and methods", "html": f"""<p><b>Index.</b> The primary index is NOAA's Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI), operational since 1 February 2026: the three-month running mean of Niño 3.4 SST anomalies minus the 20°N–20°S tropical-mean anomaly, rescaled to preserve variance {c('noaa2026', 'lheureux2024')}. The traditional ONI is reported alongside. On RONI the 1982–83 peak (+2.40, DJF 1983) is the largest of the three strongest historical events; on ONI it is +2.14. Monthly Niño indices are from ERSSTv6 (relative and absolute) and, for Niño 1+2, OISST.</p>
<p><b>Observations.</b> Contemporaneous evidence comes from moored current meters and hydrography near 0°, 110°W {c('halpern1983')}, current profiles at 159°W {c('firing1983')}, island and coastal tide gauges {c('lukas1984', 'wyrtki1985a')}, ship and island winds {c('harrison1984', 'chufrederick1990')}, and NOAA interpolated outgoing longwave radiation (OLR). Subsurface indices (warm water volume, 0–300 m heat content) for 1981–82 are reconstructions from sparse data {c('cpc', 'meinen2000')}. Satellite SST from April 1982 was biased low by up to 2.5 °C by El Chichón aerosol {c('bandeen1982')}; relative and absolute Niño indices used here are based on in situ ERSST.</p>
<p><b>Reanalysis diagnostics.</b> Daily NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis 1 winds at 1000, 850 and 200 hPa and interpolated OLR (1979–2001) were averaged over 10°S–10°N (5°S–5°N at 1000 hPa), and anomalies were formed against a 1979–2000 mean plus three annual harmonics {c('kalnay1996', 'psl')}. Westerly wind bursts are defined as a 1000 hPa anomaly ≥ 5 m s⁻¹ for ≥ 3 days over ≥ 10° of longitude within 120°E–150°W. MJO activity was assessed with five published indices — the Wheeler–Hendon RMM, ENSO-filtered RMM* (November–April only), OMI, FMO and VPM {c('wheelerhendon2004', 'kiladis2014', 'ventrice2013')} — and three documented diagnostics of our own; because these share inputs, agreement is not independent validation. Candidate episodes were tested for eastward propagation from the Indian Ocean to the western Pacific (8–35 days), a lag-correlation phase speed of 2–10 m s⁻¹, and a first-baroclinic wind structure; the full test passes in 20% of the 837 overlapping 45-day windows of January 1981 – May 1983 (and T1, T2 and T4 together in 53%), so a pass is weak evidence on its own. Indian Ocean diagnostics use ERSSTv5 boxes (western pole 50°–70°E, 10°S–10°N; south-eastern pole 90°–110°E, 10°S–0°) against 1981–2010 {c('huang2017')}.</p>
<p><b>Evidence grades.</b> A: event-specific observation plus a model or budget study isolating the effect. B: event-specific observation with a plausible mechanism and supporting studies. C: correlation, composite or general theory only. D: speculative, disputed, or untested for this event. Intermediate grades (A−, B/C) mark evidence that falls between two levels. Grades describe the link to the 1982–83 event, not the general validity of the mechanism; the confidence words used alongside them follow the evidence table.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "preconditions", "title": "3. Pre-event conditions, 1981 – early 1982", "html": f"""<p>Through 1981 the tropical Pacific was ENSO-neutral by SST: RONI stayed between −0.3 and +0.0 and was +0.11 in DJF 1982 (ONI −0.05). Beneath the surface, however, the equatorial warm-water reservoir was charged. Warm water volume was positive in every month of 1981 (+0.6 to +2.1 × 10¹⁴ m³) and reached +2.15 × 10¹⁴ m³ in March 1982, the 96th percentile of all months 1980–2025 (+1.5 standard deviations). Heat content in the upper 300 m between 180° and 100°W rose from +0.2 °C in January to +0.9 °C in March {c('cpc')}. In recharge-oscillator theory such a reservoir is the precondition that allows a westerly-forced Kelvin wave to deepen the eastern thermocline and sustain coupled growth {c('meinen2000', 'wyrtki1985b')}; the observation is event-specific but the subsurface record in 1981–82 is sparse.</p>
<p>Three further pre-conditions are documented. First, 850 hPa trades in the western Pacific were weaker than normal from February to April 1982 (−0.6 to −1.3 standardized) while Niño 1+2 remained cold (−0.7 to −1.4 °C), consistent with an onset that began in the west {c('cpc')}. Second, the tropical Pacific background state had warmed after the late 1970s; a retrospective composite of the post-1976 events shows enhanced south-east Pacific trades at onset and coastal warming that follows rather than leads central warming {c('wang1995')}. Third, the Indian Ocean's south-eastern pole was 0.3–0.5 °C cool from February 1982, before Niño 3.4 warmed, but without the equatorial easterlies that characterize a coupled dipole (Section 5.3).</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "chronology", "title": "4. Development, December 1981 – January 1983", "html": f"""<p><b>December 1981 – March 1982.</b> A strong MJO crossed from the eastern Indian Ocean into the western Pacific in late November–December 1981, consistent with the slow (about 5 m s⁻¹) eastward propagation of convection the authors found across the events they examined; an intense cold surge near 110°E preceded the eastward shift of convection by about 12 days, and low-level westerlies reached about 170°E {c('weickmannkhalsa1990')}. All five MJO indices were active (OMI 2.3, RMM 2.05). A reconstructed downwelling Kelvin wave in December 1981–January 1982 coincided with the first shift of the equatorial winds toward westerly anomalies {c('rk2007')}. A second MJO-like episode in late March 1982 passed our strict propagation test and was accompanied by a reanalysis westerly burst of up to +12 m s⁻¹ at 147.5°–165°E on 28 March–3 April. No published account of that burst was found, and the reconstruction places a Kelvin wave in March–April {c('rk2007')}.</p>
<p><b>April – May 1982.</b> RONI rose from +0.23 (FMA) to +0.74 (AMJ). On 6–16 May an 11-day westerly burst, documented from 12-hourly island, atoll and ship reports, grew from about 1000 km to over 3500 km in zonal extent, strongest over 5°N–5°S, 135°–155°E; its authors attribute it to a pressure rise in the far western equatorial Pacific following midlatitude forcing, prolonged by a pressure fall near 150°–160°E {c('chufrederick1990')}. OLR-based indices show an active MJO at the time (OMI up to 2.3), but RMM stayed between 0.6 and 1.2 until 14 May and reached 1.8 only on the burst's last day. The reconstruction finds that this burst produced only a low-amplitude Kelvin wave {c('rk2007')}.</p>
<p><b>June – July 1982: the vigorous phase begins.</b> In June a band of anomalous southerly winds formed east of Australia at 150°–160°E and crossed the equator just before sustained westerlies appeared on the equator west of the Date Line {c('harrison1984')}. Ship and island winds near the Date Line are reported to have shifted in late June from weak easterlies to westerlies of up to 8 m s⁻¹ within a month and to have persisted for months {c('harrisonschopf1984')}. The resulting downwelling Kelvin wave, directly preceded by MJO convection moving from the Indian Ocean into the western Pacific, raised Christmas Island sea level by about 25 cm in July; sea level stayed high through December {c('rk2007')}. By June relative Niño 3.4 (ERSSTv6) was +1.12 °C and the SOI −1.0. Strong anomalous warming then began along the equator in July. An advective model initialised with the July 1982 SST field and driven by the eastward currents that follow a Kelvin-wave front reproduces the amplitude and structure of the July–October anomalies; the same currents in February–April would have produced little equatorial warming because the climatological zonal SST gradient is then weak {c('harrisonschopf1984')}.</p>
<p><b>August – December 1982: coupled growth.</b> The Equatorial Undercurrent at 159°W decayed in August and partly reversed in September {c('firing1983')}; near-surface flow at 0°, 110°W reversed to eastward in August {c('halpern1983')}. Central-Pacific OLR fell to −21 W m⁻² in August, central-Pacific trades reached −2.0 standardized in September, and RONI reached +1.14 in JAS {c('cpc')}. By October SST in the equatorial zone of the eastern tropical Pacific was 5 °C above the long-term monthly mean, Galápagos sea level had risen 22 cm and the thermocline was 50–70 m deeper along the equator and the South American coast {c('halpern1983')}. Trades, SOI, convection and SST strengthened together through December, the observational signature of Bjerknes feedback {c('cpc', 'bjerknes1969')}. A coupled positive Indian Ocean Dipole developed in September–November (DMI peak +1.04 °C in October in ERSSTv5; +0.92 °C in CPC's index). Westerly bursts in October–December were the strongest of the year, but they occurred on a 60-day mean westerly anomaly of up to +5 m s⁻¹ and after the event was established {c('psl')}.</p>
<p><b>December 1982 – mid-1983: peak and discharge.</b> The first Galápagos sea-level peak occurred in December, as warm water drained from north of the equator and the east–west sea-level slope disappeared; warm water volume fell to +1.4 × 10¹⁴ m³ {c('wyrtki1985a', 'lukas1984', 'cpc')}. RONI peaked at +2.40 in DJF 1983 (ONI +2.14), with a January SOI of −3.5 and central-Pacific OLR of −46 W m⁻² {c('cpc')}. The Equatorial Undercurrent at 159°W reappeared rapidly in January 1983 {c('firing1983')}. Niño 3.4 declined from February, but a second draining episode south of the equator produced a second Galápagos sea-level peak in May and kept Niño 1+2 warm, reaching +4.0 °C in June 1983; by October 1983 sea level was below normal across the equatorial Pacific {c('wyrtki1985a', 'wyrtki1985b')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "interactions", "title": "5. Interactions with the broader climate system", "html": f"""<p><b>5.1 Intraseasonal variability and westerly wind bursts.</b> Island records show that central-Pacific easterlies weakened before each El Niño of 1950–78 except 1963, through strong westerly bursts lasting about one to three weeks {c('luther1983')}. That establishes the general mechanism but does not document 1982. For 1982 the documented events are the December 1981 MJO {c('weickmannkhalsa1990')}, the 6–16 May burst {c('chufrederick1990')}, the sustained June westerlies {c('harrison1984')}, and a succession of westerly pulses through November, known only from a later citation {c('chufrederick1990')}. A complete 1982 episode catalogue has not been assembled. With our burst definition the reanalysis contains 13 bursts in 1982 against a 1979–2001 mean of 4.2 per year (range 0–16; 1997 had 16). Part of the late-1982 count reflects the event's own slow westerly anomaly.</p>
<p>The published studies disagree on the strength of the intraseasonal forcing. Bergman et al. describe winter 1981–82 MJO activity as moderate, with three intraseasonal Kelvin waves weaker than those of 1989–90 or 1996–97 in a linear model forced by reanalysis stress, and conclude that the MJO may govern the timing and initial growth of El Niño rather than its occurrence {c('bergman2001')}. Roundy and Kiladis, using a reconstruction from island sea level, argue that 1981–83 Kelvin-wave amplitudes were larger than that wind-forced estimate and that an MJO in May–June 1982 created the environment for the burst and Kelvin wave of June–July that may have largely determined the timing and amplitude of the event {c('rk2007')}. Li et al. find eastward-propagating MJO activity in the 1982 developing stage but the weakest eastward power of the three super El Niños, and note that RMM and zonal-wind measures can disagree {c('li2023')}. Our diagnostics are consistent with all three: strong MJO-like activity in December 1981 and late March 1982, weaker activity in January–February 1982, and probable MJO-like convection in late June. The MJO is therefore graded B as a contributor to growth in June–July 1982 (one event-specific reconstruction; no model experiment isolates the effect) and unresolved for initiation. From October 1982, MJO convection reached 170°–155°W and Indian Ocean–Maritime Continent intraseasonal OLR variance was the second lowest of 22 seasons, consistent with the eastward shift and reduced activity reported for exceptional warm events {c('hendon1999', 'kessler2001', 'li2023')}: in the mature phase the influence ran from ENSO to the MJO.</p>
<p>Two candidate triggers are not supported for 1982. No twin tropical cyclogenesis occurred during the 1982–83 El Niño under either the Keen or the Harrison–Giese definition {c('schreck2009')}, although cyclone pairs produced strong westerlies in the 1970s {c('keen1982')}. The reanalysis bursts of 28 March–3 April and of 21–25 August and 27 August–3 September lack published documentation, and the August–September pair falls in a window that fails our circulation test.</p>
<p><b>5.2 El Chichón.</b> The eruptions of 29 March and 3–4 April 1982 released about 7.5 Tg of SO₂, with plumes reaching about 31 km {c('kim2025')}. Their possible contribution to initiation and to growth is judged separately. <i>Initiation.</i> The eruption followed the recharge of the warm-water reservoir, the weakening of the western-Pacific trades and the December 1981 MJO; the 28 March–3 April burst began the day before the first eruption. Khodri et al. note high equatorial heat content before the eruption and that a warm event was already under way during two of the four large historical eruptions they list {c('khodri2017')}; in GFDL CM2.1 ensembles the volcanic effect on ENSO depends on the ocean state and is unlikely to be visible when a strong El Niño is already developing at an April eruption {c('predybaylo2020')}. With three instrumental cases, Chai et al. state that post-eruption westerlies cannot be distinguished from internal variability {c('chai2020')}. A trigger role is not supported (grade D). <i>Growth.</i> Several models produce western-Pacific westerlies within months of a tropical eruption, through tropical African cooling {c('khodri2017')}, South–Southeast Asian and West African drying {c('chai2020')}, Maritime Continent cooling {c('zuo2018')}, or land drying that favours MJO propagation and more bursts {c('kim2025')}. The observed July–November 1982 signals cited for the last pathway are also normal El Niño responses, the models disagree on pathway and magnitude, and proxy records show no consistent ENSO response to eruptions over the last millennium {c('dee2020')}. A contribution to growth is possible but unresolved (grade C).</p>
<p><b>5.3 Indian Ocean.</b> The south-eastern pole cooled from February 1982 without the equatorial easterlies that drive the Indian Ocean's own Bjerknes feedback; equatorial zonal-wind anomalies over 70°–90°E were weak and changeable through mid-year (−1.0 m s⁻¹ in May, +1.0 in June, −0.4 to −0.9 in July–August) and became strongly easterly only in October–November (−2.5 to −3.1 m s⁻¹), when the DMI peaked {c('psl', 'huang2017')}. Regressing September–November DMI on Niño 3.4 over 1950–2024 (excluding 1982) predicts +0.61 °C for 1982; the observed value was +0.58 °C. The 1982 IOD was therefore as large as ENSO forcing alone would produce, consistent with the El Niño-driven loss of Maritime Continent convection {c('zhang2021')}. Feedback onto the Pacific is supported only by models: forecasts with Indian Ocean coupling suppressed underestimate the 1982–83 peak, although that model over-predicted the 1982 IOD {c('luo2010')}; IOD-induced winds in a simple model reproduce eastern-Pacific intensification of the 1972, 1982 and 1997 events {c('hameed2018')}; and decoupling the Indian and Atlantic Oceans weakens super El Niños {c('fan2024')}. One coupled model, by contrast, finds no significant Pacific impact from a fall IOD {c('ohbaueda2007')}. Rawinsonde winds show an eastward-propagating seasonal wind mode linking Indonesian convection to the Pacific; in 1982 the mode lacked the usual large autumn precursor and its amplitude and rotation began early in the year, although the authors note elements of the precursor in autumn 1981 and judge the far-western-Pacific wind evolution before 1982 not significantly different from earlier events {c('gutzlerharrison1987')}. The Indian Ocean is graded as largely a consequence, with a plausible but model-dependent amplifying feedback in late 1982 (grade C) and a possible contribution to decay through basin-wide warming in 1983 {c('ohbaueda2007', 'jourdain2016')}.</p>
<p><b>5.4 Tropical Atlantic.</b> The tropical North Atlantic cooled by 0.5–0.9 °C in August–December 1982 {c('cpc')}, a pattern usually interpreted as a lagged response to El Niño; no event-specific study of its feedback was found.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "synthesis", "title": "6. Causal synthesis and competing explanations", "html": f"""<p>The evidence supports a three-stage account. <i>Preparation</i> (1981 – early 1982): a recharged warm-water reservoir, weak western-Pacific trades from February, and intraseasonal westerly forcing in December 1981 and March 1982 set the system up but did not produce sustained warming; RONI remained below +0.5 through MAM (+0.46). <i>Triggering of the vigorous phase</i> (June–July 1982): sustained westerlies west of the Date Line, preceded by cross-equatorial southerly flow and probably favoured by an MJO, forced a downwelling Kelvin wave whose eastward currents, acting on the strong mid-year SST gradient, produced equatorial warming from July. This is the best-documented causal link in the event, resting on contemporaneous wind and current observations, an event-specific advection model and a sea-level reconstruction (grade A− for the advective mechanism; B for the role of the June westerlies and the MJO). <i>Coupled growth and discharge</i> (August 1982 – mid-1983): Bjerknes feedback amplified the anomaly to a December–January peak, after which discharge of the warm-water reservoir, in two episodes, ended the event and prolonged far-eastern warmth.</p>
<p>Three alternative emphases remain open. The first places greater weight on the December 1981 Kelvin wave as the initiating event {c('rk2007', 'weickmann1991')}; the second treats 1982 as a case of weak intraseasonal forcing in which recharge alone made onset inevitable {c('bergman2001')}; the third invokes El Chichón as an external amplifier {c('kim2025')}. The first two differ mainly in the strength of 1981–82 Kelvin waves, which the available reconstructions do not settle; the third is not supported for initiation by the timing and is not separable from internal variability for growth. Our account treats the June–July sequence as decisive because it is the only episode for which wind forcing, ocean response and subsequent SST evolution are all documented.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "knowable", "title": "7. What was knowable at the time", "html": f"""<p>Forecasters in early 1982 had the Rasmusson–Carpenter composite, in which coastal warming leads and western-Pacific easterlies strengthen before onset {c('rc1982')}. In 1982 the western-Pacific trades weakened from February, there was no early coastal warming (Niño 1+2 was −0.7 to −1.4 °C in February–April), and satellite SST was biased cold by volcanic aerosol {c('bandeen1982')}. An observer comparing early 1982 with the composite would have seen an event that did not look like El Niño. The recharge of the warm-water reservoir, now recognized as the most reliable precursor {c('meinen2000')}, could not be monitored in real time, and coupled dynamical forecasts began only with the 1986 event {c('czd1986')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "uncertainty", "title": "8. Uncertainties", "html": f"""<ul>
<li>Subsurface indices for 1981–82 are reconstructions from sparse XBT and station data; the warm-water-volume percentile is therefore uncertain.</li>
<li>Reanalysis winds in 1982 rest on sparse island and ship reports, which the Kelvin-wave reconstruction's authors describe as substantially biased {c('rk2007')}; our burst list uses a single reanalysis; an independent ERA5 re-test is set up and will be reported when it has run.</li>
<li>The Kelvin-wave reconstruction underestimates amplitudes and is the only study linking the May–June MJO to the June–July wave; the competing wind-forced estimate finds weaker 1981–82 waves {c('bergman2001')}.</li>
<li>MJO indices and our diagnostics share OLR and wind inputs; their agreement is not independent validation, and propagation-test results depend on window length.</li>
<li>Several primary sources were read only in abstract (the 1983 <i>Science</i> papers; Weickmann 1991) and one not at all (Gill and Rasmusson 1983); claims citing them are limited to what the accessible text supports (see the citation audit).</li>
<li>Satellite SST in 1982 carries aerosol and calibration biases {c('bandeen1982', 'worsfold2024')}; conclusions about SST rely on in situ ERSST.</li>
</ul>"""})

    S.append({"id": "conclusion", "title": "9. Conclusion", "html": f"""<p>The 1982–83 El Niño grew from a recharged equatorial Pacific that showed little surface signal until mid-1982. Intraseasonal westerly forcing in December 1981 and spring 1982 prepared the ocean, but the event's vigorous phase began with sustained westerlies west of the Date Line in June, a downwelling Kelvin wave in June–July and Kelvin-wave-induced advection acting on the strong mid-year zonal SST gradient. That seasonal timing explains why the event warmed first on the equator and departed from the pre-1982 composite. Bjerknes feedback carried it to a RONI peak of +2.40, and recharge–discharge dynamics ended it while prolonging warmth in the far east into mid-1983. The MJO probably contributed to growth in June–July 1982, El Chichón is not supported as a trigger, and the Indian Ocean Dipole was chiefly a response that may have amplified the peak. These grades rest on the evidence available in October 2026 and will be revised as better-constrained wind reconstructions and model experiments isolating each factor become available.</p>"""})
    return S
